\f[B]daharness\-index\f[R] \f[I]\(enstubs\f[R] [\f[I]\(encheck\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The packaged deb ships an \f[B]empty\f[R] tool registry: the SQLite
vector store \f[CR]tool_inventory\f[R] starts unpopulated and nothing
fills it for you (the services daemon never indexes \(em indexing was
removed from its boot path deliberately).
\f[B]daharness\-index\f[R] is the operator\(cqs one\-typing pass that
builds it: discovery (static AST sweep + \f[CR]\(atframework_tool\f[R]
dynamic scan) over the installed tree, embedding of every manifest
through the embed endpoint, and upsert into the registry that every
discovery lane reads \(em \f[I]tools/search\f[R],
\f[I]tools/execute\f[R] (exact\-id and semantic), \f[I]/mcp\f[R]
\f[I]tools/list\f[R], the operator REPL, and memory recall.
.PP
Before doing any work it probes its live dependency and prints the
exact fix rather than a traceback: the embed endpoint (EMBED_URL, default
http://localhost:11434/api/embed) with the embed model (default
\f[B]nomic\-embed\-text\f[R]).
Probe knobs are read from /etc/daharness/daharness.env (EMBED_MODEL,
EMBED_URL), so a conffile retune is honored.
The indexer writes to a local SQLite file (tool_registry.db under the
workspace root), so it needs filesystem write access to the state root.
.PP
Re\-runs are idempotent (upsert by tool id): newly added tools appear
without rebuilding.
\f[B]\(enclear\f[R] wipes the collection first, for from\-scratch
rebuilds.
Embedding the \(ti97\-tool universe takes on the order of a minute
against a local Ollama host.
.PP
An empty or stale registry is not an error for the gateway \(em
\f[I]tools/search\f[R] just finds nothing and \f[I]tools/execute\f[R]
404s an unknown \f[I]tool_id\f[R], which is exactly what a fresh install
looks like before this command has run once.
.SH THE STUB VIEW (\(enstubs)
\f[CR]daharness\-index \-\-stubs\f[R] generates the static\-analysis
view of the same registry: pyright stubs (a \f[CR].pyi\f[R], a PEP 562
shim, and a builtins fork) for the kernel\- and REPL\-injected tool
aliases, plus the ruff \f[CR]builtins\f[R] block for the notebook lane.
Injected names do not exist until discovery runs, so without this pass
an editor flags \f[I]every framework call\f[R] undefined \(em the stubs
close that gap from the alias/signature pipeline verbatim, so they
cannot drift from what actually executes.
.PP
This lane is \f[B]offline compute\f[R] (static AST + dynamic import \(em
no embed endpoint), so it routes \f[I]before\f[R] the index probes: a
down embed host never blocks it.
The one prerequisite it enforces itself is a complete typeshed
\f[CR]builtins.pyi\f[R] to fork (the Pylance\-extension, wheel\-pyright,
or the deb\(cqs own bundled
\f[CR]scripts/pyright_typeshed_builtins.pyi\f[R] copy) \(em refused
